# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 maple.cc _trunc. trunc cuts toward zero, but the one-argument path ended `if (is_strictly_positive(-args,...)) return -_floor(-args,...); return _floor(args,...);`, so an argument whose sign Giac cannot prove (a free name) was floored as if it were >= 0: trunc(x) = floor(x), trunc(x)-floor(x) = 0, subst(trunc(x),x=-13/10) = -2 (right -1), and a stored gg:=trunc(yy) read -2 once yy = -13/10. The two-argument path returned floor(d*x)/d (d = 10^n, a double) for either sign: trunc(-27/20,1) = -1.4 (right -1.3), trunc(-2.567,1) = -2.6 (right -2.5). Now both paths take floor(v) when v >= 0 is proven (v = x, or d*x), -floor(-v) when v < 0 is proven, each part so for a complex number (trunc(-3/2-5/2*i) = -1-2*i, was -2-3*i; iPart already cut each part toward zero), floor for undef and the infinities as before, and otherwise return trunc(x) or trunc(x,n) unevaluated, which a later substitution decides. The two-argument form still computes d*x in a double.
